    <h2 id="keyboard">Use Cmdr from the keyboard</h2>
    <p>Everything core in Cmdr works without a mouse.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Every command works from the keyboard</strong>: most have a shortcut, the rest are in the command
            palette, and Help &gt; Keyboard shortcuts lists them all in one window. You can add or change the
            shortcut of almost any command in Settings &gt; Keyboard shortcuts; a few find keys, like ⌘F, stay fixed.
            In the main window, a shortcut needs a modifier key other than Shift alone, since plain letters and
            digits, and Shift with a letter, type into the file list.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Command palette</strong>: press ⌘⇧P (Command-Shift-P) and type to find any command by name.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Switch panes</strong>: press Tab to move between the left and right file lists.</li>
        <li>
            <strong>Reach the pane controls</strong>: press ⌃F5 (Control-F5) to move focus to the focused pane's tabs,
            location bar, and sort buttons. Arrow keys move between them, and Escape takes you back to the files.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Reach the notifications</strong>: press ⌃F6 (Control-F6) to move focus to the notifications and
            their buttons, like Undo. Arrow keys and Tab move between them, and Escape takes you back.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Quick filter</strong>: while you type a filter, Space, -, and * go into the pattern. Pause for a
            moment, and they work as shortcuts again, so Space marks the files the filter left. An arrow key, Enter,
            or Tab ends the typing right away. To give yourself more time, change Settings &gt; Advanced &gt; Quick
            filter typing window.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Quick Look</strong>: ⇧Space (Shift-Space) opens it, even while a quick filter is on.</li>
        <li>
            <strong>Find in an Ask Cmdr chat</strong>: with focus in the Ask Cmdr panel, press ⌘F to search the
            conversation. Enter or ⌘G goes to the next match, ⇧Enter or ⇧⌘G to the previous one, and Escape closes
            the find bar and returns you to where you were.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Dialogs that open by themselves</strong>, like a question from a running copy, don't take the
            keys you're typing elsewhere. Stop typing for a moment and the dialog's keys start working; press Tab to
            reach a text field. Escape works right away.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Undo a move to trash</strong> any time from Edit &gt; Undo move to trash, after its notification is
            gone. The menu item names what it puts back, like "Undo move to trash of 3 items".
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Move tabs</strong>: ⌘⌃⇧← and ⌘⌃⇧→ (Command-Control-Shift-arrow) move the current tab along its bar,
            and "Move tab to other pane" in the command palette sends it across.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Single-key shortcuts</strong> in the file viewer (like W for word wrap and 1 for text view) can be
            changed or turned off in Settings &gt; Keyboard shortcuts, under File viewer.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Full Keyboard Access</strong>: in the main window, Tab switches panes, so macOS Full Keyboard Access
            doesn't move through the pane controls. Use ⌃F5 for those. Menus are native macOS menus, so they work as
            usual.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="screen-readers">Screen readers</h2>
    <p>
        Cmdr works with VoiceOver through the macOS accessibility interface, and VoiceOver's own keyboard commands pass
        through.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Works today</strong>: dialogs, Settings, and forms are built for screen readers, and dialogs keep
            and return focus correctly. The menu bar and context menus are native, so VoiceOver reads them like any
            other app's. Notifications are read out without moving your focus. A dialog that opens by itself says that
            the keyboard waits until you stop typing, so a keystroke meant for something else can't answer it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>The file list</strong>: as the cursor moves, VoiceOver reads the file's name, its
            kind (folder, symlink, package, or archive), and its position, like "3 of 8", and says "selected" for marked
            files. Switching panes or folders says which pane and folder you're in, and in Full view each file also
            says its size and date.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>In-app menus</strong>, like the volume switcher (⌥F1) and Favorites, read each row
            as you arrow through them, submenus included.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Tested with VoiceOver</strong>: a hands-on test heard the file list, switching panes, marking
            files, the in-app menus, the copy progress dialog, renaming, Search, Settings, the delete confirmation,
            notifications, and the file viewer read as described here. The
            <a href="/trust/accessibility">conformance report</a> has the details, criterion by criterion. If
            something reads wrong for you, please say so (see <a href="#help">Get help</a>).
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="seeing">Text size, zoom, and colors</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Text size</strong>: set it from 75% to 200% in Settings &gt; Appearance &gt; Zoom and density &gt;
            Text size, or with ⌘+ and ⌘− (Command-plus and Command-minus; ⌘0 resets it). It scales the whole app, rows
            included, and adds to the macOS text size in System Settings &gt; Accessibility, so text can go further.
        </li>
        <li><strong>macOS Zoom</strong> works with Cmdr like with any other app.</li>
        <li>
            <strong>Light and dark mode</strong>: Settings &gt; Appearance &gt; Theme mode. Pick Light, Dark, or
            System to follow macOS.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>System font and accent color</strong>: Cmdr uses the macOS system font and your accent color.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Contrast</strong>: text contrast is checked automatically in light and dark mode, with every macOS
            accent color. Turn on Increase contrast in System Settings &gt; Accessibility &gt; Display, and Cmdr
            draws stronger borders, secondary text, focus rings, and pane tints.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Marked files and the focused pane</strong>: marked files carry a bar down their left edge and
            slightly heavier text besides their red color. The cursor has an accent-colored outline in the focused pane
            and a gray one in the other pane, and the focused pane's active tab has the stronger accent line along its
            top. These cues are subtle by default; turn on Differentiate without color or Increase contrast in the same
            place, and the other pane's outline turns dashed, so you can tell the panes apart without color, and the
            outlines get stronger.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Show borders</strong>: turn it on in System Settings &gt; Accessibility &gt; Display, and Cmdr's
            buttons that have no edge of their own, like a tab's close button, the volume chip, and the path segments,
            get a visible border.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Tooltips</strong> stay up while you move the pointer onto them, so you can read them with the
            screen magnified. Any key closes them.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="motion">Motion and transparency</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Reduce motion</strong>: turn it on in System Settings &gt; Accessibility &gt; Display, and Cmdr's
            activity indicators, like spinners, stop moving. Cmdr has no flashing content, and no audio or video.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Reduce transparency</strong>: turn it on in the same place, and Cmdr's frosted-glass surfaces turn
            solid.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="notifications">Time to read notifications</h2>
    <p>
        Notifications go away after a few seconds by default. To keep them up until you close them, turn on Settings
        &gt; Behavior &gt; Notifications &gt; "Keep notifications until I close them". A notification
        also stays while your pointer or keyboard focus is on it. Missed a one-time tip? Each one has a switch in
        Settings &gt; Advanced &gt; Hints and warnings: turn it off, and the tip shows again the next time it applies.
    </p>

    <h2 id="gaps">Known gaps</h2>
    <p>
        Cmdr doesn't fully meet WCAG 2.2 Level AA yet. These open issues track what's missing, and each one is public,
        so you can follow the fix:
    </p>
    <ul>
        {
            gapIssues.map((issue) => (
                <li>
                    <a href={issueUrl(issue.number)}>#{issue.number}</a>: {issue.title}
                </li>
            ))
        }
    </ul>

    <h2 id="help">Get help</h2>
    <p>
        Need something to work differently, or hit a wall? Write to
        <a href="mailto:hello@getcmdr.com">hello@getcmdr.com</a>, or use Help &gt; Send feedback in the app.
        Accessibility questions and reports are welcome, and every one gets an answer. If something blocks you, say
        what you use (for example VoiceOver, Zoom, or a switch device) and what you tried, so the fix can target it.
    </p>
